{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"paediatric-orchestral-anemone","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Problem\n\nDiscovered during the Step-7 sibling sweep of baldish-regainable-steed (e4a6-545b-36af-456d), which fixed the tickets-branch CAS push to back off with JITTER (not lockstep) before re-fetching.\n\n`src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/last_pass.py` publishes the last-pass witness under a 3-attempt CAS loop:\n\n```python\nfor attempt in range(3):\n    if ref_lock._cas_advance(repo_root, LAST_PASS_REF, new_oid=new_oid, old_oid=old_oid, remote=remote):\n        ...\n        return record\n    if attempt == 2:\n        break\n    sleeper((0.1, 0.2)[attempt])          # FIXED, unjittered schedule\n    old_oid = ref_lock._ref_oid(repo_root, LAST_PASS_REF, remote=remote) or _ZERO_OID\n```\n\nThis loop is STALENESS-correct (it re-reads `old_oid` after the sleep, so the next CAS is against fresh state — unlike the baldish tickets-push bug). But the backoff schedule `(0.1, 0.2)` is deterministic and unjittered: two reconciler passes that collide once on LAST_PASS_REF sleep identical durations, wake together, and re-collide — the same thundering-herd/lockstep class as the JITTER half of baldish.\n\n## Scope / why not fixed in the baldish change\n\nThis is a DIFFERENT subsystem (the reconciler ref-lock publication) with its own concurrency model — reconciler passes are more serialized than tickets writes, so the exposure is lower and the fix warrants its own review rather than being folded into the tickets-push bugfix. The staleness half does NOT apply here (the loop already re-reads after the sleep).\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] The last-pass CAS publication backoff is jittered (additive jitter on the base schedule) so colliding reconciler passes do not wake in lockstep, mirroring the additive-jitter idiom now used by the tickets-push `_cas_backoff` (`src/rebar/_store/push_classify.py`) and the Jira DC transport.\n- [ ] The staleness-correct re-read of `old_oid` after the sleep is preserved.\n- [ ] A test drives colliding publications and asserts the backoff delays vary (not a single lockstep value), with a negative control at zero contention.\n\n## Notes on adjacent non-siblings (from the same sweep, recorded so they are not re-triaged)\n\n- `_TRANSPORT_BACKOFF_SECONDS` (push_classify.py) rides out a transient TLS fault, not writer contention — thundering-herd does not apply, jitter would not help convergence.\n- `_coordinated_fetch`'s `_FETCH_CAS_BACKOFF_S` (sync.py) re-fetches after the sleep (staleness-correct) and serializes rebar peers behind `fetch_coordination_lock`, so rebar-peer lockstep is already prevented; the residual racer is a non-rebar git peer.","id":"ad7b-6928-49fe-475a","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"last-pass CAS publication retry uses an unjittered fixed backoff (0.1, 0.2), so competing reconciler passes re-collide in lockstep"},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1787963323991095001,"uuid":"0575896a-9d71-4c52-8c0c-d596e3f0cf27"}